Foreign formal-language content a DSL owns — mathematics and code, embedded when small and exhibited when large.

<section #sh-align | `<align>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *align*. Compiles to eHTML `<align>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<align>`

<code-block #code:sh-align-1>
<align>
x^2 + y^2 &= z^2 \\
a + b &= c
</align>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-align-1>
<align>
x^2 + y^2 &= z^2 \\
a + b &= c
</align>
</minipage>

*Two aligned equations. The `&amp;` marks the alignment column (here,
the `=` sign). Handler wraps in `\begin{aligned}...\end{aligned}`
(KaTeX-compatible variant of LaTeX's `align`).*

</section>

<section #sh-cases | `<cases>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *cases*. Compiles to eHTML `<cases>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<cases>`

<code-block #code:sh-cases-1>
<cases>
x^2 & \text{if } x \ge 0 \\
-x^2 & \text{if } x < 0
</cases>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-cases-1>
<cases>
x^2 & \text{if } x \ge 0 \\
-x^2 & \text{if } x < 0
</cases>
</minipage>

*A two-case piecewise definition. Handler wraps in
`\begin{cases}...\end{cases}`.*

</section>

<section #sh-code | `<code>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *code*. Compiles to eHTML `<code>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<code>`

<code #code:sh-code-1>
The function is `factorial`.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-code-1>
The function is `factorial`.
</minipage>

*Plain markdown backticks produce inline &lt;code&gt;. The most common
authoring path.*

<code #code:sh-code-2>
Use `<`code`>` for inline code.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-code-2>
Use `<`code`>` for inline code.
</minipage>

*The enscribe sigil form. Equivalent to plain markdown backticks
but supports attributes.*

<code #code:sh-code-3>
<code language=python | def hello(): print("hi")>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-code-3>
<code language=python | def hello(): print("hi")>
</minipage>

<code #code:sh-code-4>
<code #factorial-impl language=python | def factorial(n):>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-code-4>
<code #factorial-impl language=python | def factorial(n):>
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `language` | kwarg |  | The programming language of the code. |
| `src` | kwarg |  | An @id reference (#313 consumer wiring) that pulls a stored &lt;dataset&gt; declared in &lt;data&gt; and renders its opaque bytes as the verbatim code body — e.g. |

</section>

<section #sh-code-block | `<code-block>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *code-block*. Compiles to eHTML `<code-block>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<code-block>`

<code #code:sh-code-block-1>
<``` python | print("hello, world") ```>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-code-block-1>
<``` python | print("hello, world") ```>
</minipage>

*The triple-backtick sigil. The first positional token is the
language (emitted as a `language-X` class on the &lt;code&gt;); the pipe
separates it from the verbatim, whitespace-preserving content — the
pipe-form padding (the spaces around the content) is KEPT, since
whitespace in code is significant and visible to the reader. (#327)*

<code #code:sh-code-block-2>
<``` this is all content ```>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-code-block-2>
<``` this is all content ```>
</minipage>

*The no-pipe form: the entire body is opaque content with no language
extraction.*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `src` | kwarg |  | An @id reference (Option A / #313) that pulls a stored &lt;dataset&gt; declared in &lt;data&gt; and renders its opaque bytes as the code block's verbatim body — e.g. |

</section>

<section #sh-display-math | `<display-math>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *display-math*. Compiles to eHTML `<display-math>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<display-math>`

<code #code:sh-display-math-1>
<$$ \sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i = X $$>
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-display-math-1>
<$$ \sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i = X $$>
</minipage>

*The `$$` sigil. Display-mode LaTeX rendered by KaTeX on its own line;
numbered by default (the equation number is appended after the KaTeX
output).*

<sub-section | Arguments>

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `+numbered` / `-numbered` | flag | default on | Whether this equation participates in the document-wide numbered sequence. |

</section>

<section #sh-eqnarray | `<eqnarray>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *eqnarray*. Compiles to eHTML `<eqnarray>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<eqnarray>`

<code-block #code:sh-eqnarray-1>
<eqnarray>
f(x) &=& x^2 \\
g(x) &=& 2x
</eqnarray>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-eqnarray-1>
<eqnarray>
f(x) &=& x^2 \\
g(x) &=& 2x
</eqnarray>
</minipage>

*Two equations rendered via KaTeX's `aligned` env (the supported
equivalent of LaTeX's `eqnarray`).*

</section>

<section #sh-inline-code | `<inline-code>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *inline-code*. Compiles to eHTML `<inline-code>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<inline-code>`

<code #code:sh-inline-code-1>
Assign with <` x = 1 `> at the top of the function.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-inline-code-1>
Assign with <` x = 1 `> at the top of the function.
</minipage>

*The single-backtick sigil. With no pipe, the whole body is opaque
code content rendered as &lt;code&gt; (the same output as a markdown
backtick span). Whitespace in code is significant and preserved, so
the pipe-form padding stays INSIDE the &lt;code&gt;. (#327)*

<code #code:sh-inline-code-2>
Call <` python | factorial(n) `> to recurse.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-inline-code-2>
Call <` python | factorial(n) `> to recurse.
</minipage>

*The first positional token before the pipe is the language, emitted
as a `language-X` class on the &lt;code&gt; (discoverable by highlighters;
the interpreter applies no highlighting itself).*

</section>

<section #sh-inline-math | `<inline-math>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *inline-math*. Compiles to eHTML `<inline-math>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<inline-math>`

<code #code:sh-inline-math-1>
The identity <$ a^2 + b^2 = c^2 $> holds for right triangles.
</code>

<minipage #mp:sh-inline-math-1>
The identity <$ a^2 + b^2 = c^2 $> holds for right triangles.
</minipage>

*The `$` sigil. Opaque LaTeX content rendered inline by KaTeX and
wrapped in &lt;inline-math&gt; for CSS targeting. The sigil carries no
attributes — id and classes are not supported for inline math.*

</section>

<section #sh-math | `<math>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *math*. Compiles to eHTML `<math>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<math>`

<code-block #code:sh-math-1>
<math>
E = mc^2
</math>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-math-1>
<math>
E = mc^2
</math>
</minipage>

*Long-form `&lt;math&gt;` block. Semantically equivalent to the
`&lt;$$ E = mc^2 $$&gt;` display-math sigil — both render block-level
LaTeX math via KaTeX. Use the long-form when the source is
multi-line or when explicit tag bounds aid readability; use the
sigil for brevity.*

</section>

<section #sh-matrix | `<matrix>`>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Semantic role: *matrix*. Compiles to eHTML `<matrix>`.

Registers:

- Canonical: `<matrix>`

<code-block #code:sh-matrix-1>
<matrix>
1 & 2 \\
3 & 4
</matrix>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:sh-matrix-1>
<matrix>
1 & 2 \\
3 & 4
</matrix>
</minipage>

*A 2×2 matrix. The handler wraps the body in
`\begin{matrix}...\end{matrix}` before KaTeX renders.*

</section>
